  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nTemperatures are forecast to gradually warm with above normal temps \nby the middle of the week. This is due to a mid/upper level ridge of \nhigh pressure and a modifying airmass.\n\nAs for rain chances, deep mid and upper level moisture associated \nwith an upper level short wave currently tracking east across S TX \nhas brought light rain to a few locations  may continue through \ntoday. In addition, low level moisture (PWATs 1.8 inches) moving \ntoward the coastline from the gulf associated with a surface to mid \nlevel trough, will bring a low to very low chance of showers along \nthe immediate coast. However, much of S TX will remain dry. Higher \nchances are expected across the coastal waters where deeper moisture \nwill reside. Models show drier conditions (PWATs around 0.8in) \nmoving into S TX Tuesday, further limiting any rain chances. Models \nthen show PWATs of 1.8 inches moving back into S TX on Wednesday. \nThe surface trough along the coast is progged to shift west \nWednesday, thus the low chance of showers will also shift west. Rain \nchances are higher across Deep S TX. Rain chances decrease to very \nlow or nil the latter part of the week as the mid/upper level high \npressure becomes more dominant across the region. Latest model runs \nindicate the next chance for precipitation to occur is early next \nweek with another surface to mid level trough moving west across the \nregion.\n\nModels indicate that a northeast to east wind will persist across \nthe gulf waters through Thursday due to the series of surface to mid \nlevel troughs moving west across the region. Although the flow will \ngenerally be 5-15 knots, the persistent direction and swell periods \naround 8 seconds could lead to minor coastal flooding during times \nof high tide. P-ETSS indicates that tide levels will remain below \nthe 2ft MSL threshold, thus confidence is low, but will continue to \nmonitor trends.\n\nAlthough the northeast flow tends to limit the rip current risk to a \nlow risk, the 8 second period swells makes it hard to go with a low \nrisk. Thus bumped it to moderate Today through Tuesday.",
